<h1>Privacy</h1>
<p class="text-muted">Agendal is a student project for the Advanced Web Design course at VNU-KAIST. This page says, in plain words, what the app stores about you and why. Last updated 18 October 2026.</p>

<h2>What we store</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Your account:</strong> email address, name, a hashed password (never the password itself), and your time zone and appearance settings.</li>
  <li><strong>Your work:</strong> the tasks, projects, habits and reminders you create, and the profile photo if you upload one (resized to 256 px).</li>
  <li><strong>Google sign-in:</strong> if you use it, your Google account ID, email and name. We never see your Google password.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why</h2>
<p>Only to run the app for you: to sign you in, show your own tasks on any device, and send the emails you ask for, such as a password reset link. Your data is private to your account. We don’t sell it or use it for advertising.</p>

<h2>Cookies</h2>
<p>Agendal sets one cookie, which keeps you signed in for up to 30 days (or until you close the browser if you untick “Remember me”). The app needs it to work, so there is no cookie banner. There are no tracking or advertising cookies.</p>

<h2>Other services</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The Inter font is loaded from Google Fonts, so your browser contacts Google when a page opens.</li>
  <li>Emails are sent through the mail server the app’s operators configure.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Your choices</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Change your name, photo and time zone in <a href="/settings/profile">Settings</a>, and your password in <a href="/settings/account">Account</a>.</li>
  <li>Logging out ends your sessions on every device.</li>
  <li>To have your account and all its data deleted, ask the team on the project’s <a href="https://github.com/khang-ngo4444/VCD_Agendal">GitHub page</a>. A self-service delete button is planned.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Security</h2>
<p>Passwords are hashed and salted, connections are encrypted with HTTPS in production, and repeated wrong passwords temporarily lock an account. No system is perfectly secure. If you find a problem, please tell us on GitHub.</p>
